<blockquote>
<p>I'm also iffy on the described effects here. I'm not sure a person would feel pain due to this. I doubt very much that slowing down the speed of cognition would have the same effects as laughing gas or intoxication, because from the frame of reference of the person that's slowed, there would be no difference. An outside observer would note they appeared to move/think/speak/whatever more slowly, but that doesn't equate to impaired mental function, I don't think.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think lowering the speed of light significantly could have weird effects on people. Mostly because different parts of the body are all moving at different speeds. Your heart is pumping, blood is moving, your lungs are inflating/deflating. If c is low enough that your blood is moving at 0.95c, while some other part of your body is moving at 0.50c, and you would probably have some strange side effects. Not that they would be the same as what's stated in the text, but weird stuff is plausible I think. Hell, slow it enough that air currents are moving at a significant percentage of the speed of light, and maybe the person would get torn to pieces.</p>

