					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/adam-smascher" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(587467); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=587467&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1605988453" alt="Adam Smascher" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=587467)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/adam-smascher" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(587467); return false;" >Adam Smascher</a></span> <span class="odate time_1300742153 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Mar 2011 21:15</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1072799">
									

<p>I'm not thinking time dilation. What I mean by the impaired cognition is that, to someone outside the chamber, you may appear to be moving slower. However, this doesn't translate to impaired mental function for the person <em>inside</em> the chamber.</p>
<p>As for the baseball thing, I mean yeah, it would mess your arm up. Probably, if you could do it. But I think it'd be more than just pain, and as I said, I'm not sure that you'd be able to move that fast anyway. Slow the speed of light, does everything else slow accordingly? In that thrown baseball example, the neural impulses responsible for motor control would travel at superluminal speeds. That's a big no-no as far as physics goes for a number of reasons, not the least of which is that electrons are more massive than photons, unless I'm mistaken.</p>
<p>I think that, from the perspective of someone outside the chamber, <em>everything</em> inside would appear to have slowed, as opposed to now occurring at significant percentages of <em>c</em>. The person inside would, assuming it wasn't outright fatal, feel no difference.</p>
<p>I guess that the effect may be similar to time dilation, even though the person in the chamber is stationary relative to the outside observer's frame of reference. A light clock inside the chamber would, to an outside observer, appear to tick more slowly. To the person inside the chamber, there would be no appreciable difference. Because of this and the other points I've outlined, I do not think the results of exposure to such an environment where <em>c</em> is reduced would be as described. I'm almost certain of this.</p>

							</div>

								
					
